<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Wu Zhou</h1><p class="st-meta">690 CE – 705 CE · capital Shendu (Luoyang)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Zhou dynasty proclaimed by Wu Zetian, the only woman in Chinese history to rule as emperor in her own name, interrupting the Tang from 690 to 705.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-zetian">Wu Zhao</a>, a concubine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Emperor Taizong</a>, became empress to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozong-of-tang">Emperor Gaozong</a> in 655 and gradually took over government as he fell ill. After his death in 683 she deposed her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-zhongzong-of-tang">Zhongzong</a>, kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-ruizong-of-tang">Ruizong</a> as a figurehead, crushed a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> princes' revolt and in 690 proclaimed her own <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/western-zhou">Zhou dynasty</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, renamed Shendu, as capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-zetian">Wu Zetian</a> promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, using the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-cloud-sutra">Great Cloud Sutra</a> to support a female ruler, and favoured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maitreya">Maitreya</a> cult. She widened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-examination">examination system</a> to recruit men from outside the old north-western aristocracy, invented new written characters and relied on informers and secret police such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lai-junchen">Lai Junchen</a>. The frontier wars against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-turkic-khaganate">Second Turkic Khaganate</a>, Khitan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> had mixed results.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 705 ministers led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-jianzhi">Zhang Jianzhi</a> killed her favourites, the Zhang brothers, and forced the aged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-zetian">Wu Zetian</a> to abdicate in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-zhongzong-of-tang">Zhongzong</a>, restoring the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a>. She died later that year and was buried with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozong-of-tang">Gaozong</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianling-mausoleum">Qianling mausoleum</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/wu-zhou.jpg" alt="Wu Zhou" width="320" height="309"><figcaption>Image: Ian Kiu · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tang_Dynasty_circa_700_CE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>690 CE – 705 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shendu (Luoyang)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wu Zetian</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zhou dynasty (690-705), Second Zhou</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=698&amp;focus=wu-zhou" aria-label="Wu Zhou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7423 962 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Wu Zhou lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7771 1192h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7743 1218h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7743 1218h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=698&amp;focus=wu-zhou">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
